Q: Console not handling escape sequences A: <p>Just in case someone gets to this page after 5+ years of the question being posted, this worked quite decently for me:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>ShellStream shellStreamSSH = _sshClient.CreateShellStream(&quot;vt320&quot;, 80, 60, 800, 600, 65536);

var readTask = Task.Run(() =&gt;
{
    while (true)
    {
        if (shellStreamSSH.DataAvailable)
        {
            Console.Write((char)shellStreamSSH.ReadByte());
        }
    }
});

var writeTask = Task.Run(() =&gt;
{
    while (true)
    {
        if (shellStreamSSH.CanWrite)
        {
            shellStreamSSH.Write(Console.ReadKey(true).KeyChar.ToString());
        }
    }
});

await Task.WhenAll(readTask, writeTask);
</code></pre>
<p>In case it matters: It was tested by ssh'ing to an Alpine Docker container.</p>

Q: How to validate that a string is of a desired value with integers in certain positions in C? A: <blockquote>
<p>a second opinion to see if my implementation works</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It does not.</p>
<p><code>fgets(buffer, size, stdin)</code> reads up to <code>size - 1</code> characters (which includes the <code>'\n'</code>) and then appends a <em>null character</em> to the buffer.</p>
<p>Consider:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>printf(&quot;Enter the task's due date (DD/MM/YYYY):\n&quot;);
    if (fgets(aux_date, 10, stdin) == NULL){
</code></pre>
<p>OP's code instead attempts to read an 11 character text input like <code>&quot;03/01/2026\n&quot;</code> (which needs a buffer of at least 12 to store as a <em>string</em>.)</p>
<p>Further, if the line input is too long, only a partial amount is read with the rest left for the next input function..  That incomplete read is not yet detected.</p>
<p>Instead, allocate a buffer, perhaps 2x the anticipated needed size to consume extras.</p>
<p>Also perform testing in a logical sequence (perhaps with helper functions):</p>
<ul>
<li>Did we get the line?,</li>
<li>Is it numeric with expected separators?</li>
<li>Range of values <em>roughly</em> OK?</li>
<li>Did we reject other spurious dates like <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Year_1900_problem#Microsoft_Excel" rel="nofollow noreferrer">29/02/1900</a>? (This was not done below.)</li>
</ul>
<p>Sample illustrative code:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#define LINE_SZ_MAX 100
char buf[LINE_SZ_MAX * 2];

if (fgets(buf, sizeof buf, stdin) == NULL) {
  // No more input (or rare input error)
  ...
  exit(-1);
}

size_t len = strlen(buf);
if (len + 1 == sizeof buf) {
  // Input was too long.
  // We could take evasive action, but for simplicity,
  // just bail out with hostile input.
  ...
  exit(-1);
}

struct tm tm = { 0 }; // Zero all members.

// [Added later]
// A particular aggressive one liner test to &quot;validate that a string is
// of a desired value with integers in certain positions&quot;.
// Research the role of %n.
// This sscanf can only passes with 2-digts / 2-digits / 4-digits (opt \n)
int n = 0;
sscanf(buf, &quot;%*1d%*d/%*1d%*d/%*1d%*1d%*1d%*d%n&quot;, &amp;n);
if (n != 10 || (buf[n] != '\n' &amp;&amp; buf[n] != '\0')) {
  // Invalid numeric input
  ...
}

// Gently parse input for date.
// For simplicity, tolerate optional leading white space and signs and the final \n.
// Unless truly needed, consider being tolerant.
n = 0;
sscanf(buf, &quot;%2d/%2d/%4d %n&quot;, &amp;tm.tm_mday, &amp;tm.tm_mon, &amp;tm.tm_year, &amp;n);
if (n == 0 || buf[n] != '\0') {
  // Invalid numeric input
  ...
}

// Perform some range tests.  Augment as desired.
if (tm.tm_mday &lt; 1 || tm.tm_mday &gt; 31 || tm.tm_mon &lt; 1 || tm.tm_mon &gt; 12) {
  // Invalid date
  ...
}

printf(&quot;Date: %02d/%02d/%04d\n&quot;, tm.tm_mday, tm.tm_mon, tm.tm_year);

// Adjust to struct tm members' epochs.
tm.tm_mon--;  // Months since January
tm.tm_year -= 1900  // Years since 1900
</code></pre>
<blockquote>
<p>algorithm more robust, secure and efficient.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>An important missing goal: it is maintainable and clear?</p>
<p>Robust code's goals evolve over time.  With parsing a <em>date</em>, we really do not need that much efficiency.  I'd go for maintainable code.  Review <a href="https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/q/80084/94903">Is premature optimization really the root of all evil?</a>.</p>
